land-based ballistic missiles  submarine-launched weaponry and bombs  that can be deployed from aircraft  these are some of the weapons in  russia's vast nuclear arsenal seen here  on display in training drills before  russia launched its invasion of ukraine  the strategic systems they have  intercontinental range and so they can  hit anywhere on the globe while the us  doesn't communicate much on its nuclear  weapons russia has been showcasing its  advances  after president putin ordered moscow's  nuclear deterrence forces to be put on  alert in late february the pentagon said  it had not detected any major new  developments on that front  many analysts say it's unlikely the  weapons will be used in warfare although  it can't be ruled out but moscow could  use its arsenal as a deterrent to  discourage the us and its allies from  taking more action in the ukraine war so  here's what we know about russia's  nuclear inventory the largest in the  world and putin's push to modernize it  when it comes to scale russia and the us  have a similar number of nuclear weapons  according to an estimate by the  federation of american scientists  russia's warhead inventory totals 5977  in comparison the us has 5428  those numbers are similar  also due to international arms control  agreements to limit the size of each  country's nuclear stockpile at the heart  of moscow's arsenal is what military  experts call the nuclear triad the u.s  also has nuclear triad status while  china has been working to achieve the  same advanced version in recent years  in principle russia's weapons can be  delivered by air with strategic bombers  such as the  tu-95ms and tu-160  by its navy with nuclear-powered  ballistic missile submarines and by  land-based ballistic launchers such as  this rs 24 yars the explosive force of  atomic bombs is measured in kilotons and  warheads on these weapons have a power  of as much as 400 kilotons according to  defense analysts  that's more than 25 times the atomic  bomb the us used in hiroshima japan at  the end of world war ii  days after the first bomb was released  another was dropped on nagasaki seen  here in this footage in 1945. it became  the last atomic bomb to be used in  combat since then countries have built  up arsenals as a deterrent for conflict  the u.s and russia created their vast  stockpiles during the cold war  but our restraint  is not inexhaustible more recently and  decades after the end of that cold war  moscow is again touting that its  intercontinental ballistic missiles are  capable of hitting the us in 20 to 30  minutes hans christensen is the director  of the nuclear information project and  has been studying nuclear weapons for  decades he says long-range missiles are  the most powerful elements in russia's  arsenal and if that was released  it could destroy all major cities  the u.s also has missiles capable of  potentially hitting russia within a  similar time frame  in addition both countries have  stockpiles of non-strategic nuclear  weapons which are usually defined as  shorter range delivery systems with  lower yield warheads but analysts note  as the us has been scaling back its  stockpile of these weapons over the last  three decades russia has largely kept  its inventory we think the us is only a  small inventory of a few hundred  tactical nuclear bombs that are left  this has been an important development  in the u.s  arsenal evolution because they used to  have like russia a huge inventory of  non-strategic but they've largely moved  away from that in the u.s  planning  russia is modernizing some soviet aero  weapons which analysts say is a routine  step in order to maintain the readiness  of older tools it's also developing  newer weapons that have been publicized  by russian president vladimir putin and  on russian state tv  zircon is one of six nuclear weapons  putin revealed the country was  developing in march 2018  others included underwater  nuclear-powered uncrewed vehicles and  nuclear-powered global range cruise  missiles  analysts say it's hard to determine what  stage of development they are currently  at  unlike the us russia has been sharing  its nuclear capabilities on state tv and  online media  wary of stoking tensions as war rages in  ukraine u.s officials even canceled a  scheduled weapon test although other  exercises continued  in mid-march the us air force conducted  drills with b-52 bombers capable of  carrying nuclear missiles  but to use nuclear weapons would be a  significant step  some experts say putin may turn to  non-strategic bombs if cornered in this  war however the bar is high for any  potential deployment and it's unlikely  that russia will use them at all  it's one thing to rattle the sword and  warn your adversaries that you have this  capability by doing exercises or  increasing alert or something like that  it's another  it's another step to actually use  nuclear weapons because  once you do all bets are off in terms of  how  destructive this  new phase is going to be the build up  and showcasing of new weapons during the  ukraine war reminds some of the cold war  era a period when the u.s and russia  used ownership of nuclear forces as a  defense amid heightened tensions  [Music]
 

